# Functions

## Authentication

### get_headers

`get_headers(app_id: str, app_secret: str, tenant_id: str) -> dict`

Acquires an OAuth2 Bearer token using client credentials and returns an `Authorization` header dictionary. The token is scoped to `https://graph.microsoft.com/.default`.

Raises `GraphAuthError` if the token request fails at the network level, if the token endpoint returns a non-JSON body, or if the response carries no `access_token` — in the last case the message includes the Azure AD `error` code and `error_description`.

```python
headers = get_headers(app_id, app_secret, tenant_id)
```

### get_headers_from_vault

`get_headers_from_vault(api_key: str, credential_id: int | str) -> dict`

Same result as `get_headers`, for app registrations stored in Password State. Fetches the credential through `InternetCredentialManager.get_credential_from_id`, takes the tenant ID from the last path segment of the entry's `URL`, and calls `get_headers` with `UserName` as the app id and `Password` as the secret.

Raises `GraphAuthError` if the credential cannot be retrieved from the vault, and propagates the `GraphAuthError` from `get_headers` if the token request itself fails.

```python
headers = get_headers_from_vault(vault_internet_api_key, credential_id)
```

### GraphCredentials

`class GraphCredentials`

An app registration's identity, from which tokens are minted on demand. Pass an
instance anywhere a helper takes `headers` and every request it makes gains
token-expiry handling. Use it for any process that may outlive a single token;
`get_headers` and `get_headers_from_vault` remain the right choice for
short-lived callers.

Constructed through one of two named constructors:

- `GraphCredentials.from_vault(api_key: str, credential_id: int | str)` — for app registrations stored in Password State. The vault is re-read on every mint, so a rotated client secret is picked up by an already-running process instead of stranding it.
- `GraphCredentials.from_app_registration(app_id: str, app_secret: str, tenant_id: str)` — for callers who already hold these values and would otherwise call `get_headers` directly.

Members:

- `headers -> dict` — the live headers dictionary. It is the same object for the life of the credentials and is updated **in place** on a re-mint, so a closure, a queued coroutine, or an `asyncio.to_thread` call already in flight keeps working without being handed a replacement. Reach for this only when calling Graph outside this library, where nothing will re-mint on your behalf.
- `generation -> int` — how many tokens this object has minted.
- `refresh(seen_generation: int) -> int` — re-mint, unless another caller has already replaced the token that `seen_generation` refers to.
- `call(operation)` — run one Graph operation, re-minting once on a 401. An escape hatch for Graph calls this library does not wrap; anything going through a `sharepoint`, `mail`, or `subscription` helper needs no wrapping. `operation` must be safe to run twice.

Instances are safe to share between threads: concurrent callers that all met the
same expired token produce a single re-mint between them, not one each. Minting
is retried on transient failure and raises `GraphAuthError` when it gives up.

```python
from wcp_library.graph import GraphCredentials
from wcp_library.graph.sharepoint import list_folder

credentials = GraphCredentials.from_vault(vault_internet_api_key, credential_id)
items = list_folder(credentials, site_id, "/Shared Documents")

# For a Graph endpoint this library does not wrap:
result = credentials.call(lambda headers: my_own_graph_call(headers, ...))
```

### GraphAuthError

`class GraphAuthError(Exception)`

Raised when Microsoft Graph token acquisition fails, by either `get_headers` or `get_headers_from_vault`. Distinct from the `requests.RequestException` family raised by the Graph data helpers, so authentication problems can be separated from call failures.

```python
from wcp_library.graph import GraphAuthError, get_headers_from_vault

try:
    headers = get_headers_from_vault(vault_internet_api_key, credential_id)
except GraphAuthError as exc:
    logger.error("Could not authenticate against Graph: %s", exc)
    raise
```

## SharePoint — Site Functions

### get_site_metadata

`get_site_metadata(headers: dict, site_home_url: str) -> dict`

Retrieve site metadata from a SharePoint site URL. The URL must be the site home page.

```python
get_site_metadata(headers, "https://contoso.sharepoint.com/sites/MySite")
```

### get_drives

`get_drives(headers: dict, site_id: str, *, page_size: int | None = None) -> list[dict]`

List document libraries (drives) on a SharePoint site. Follows `@odata.nextLink` across all pages. Raises `requests.RequestException` on HTTP errors or exhausted retries. An optional `page_size` tunes the Graph `$top` value on the initial request.

```python
drives = get_drives(headers, site_id)
for drive in drives:
    print(drive["name"], drive["id"])
```

### get_drive_id_by_name

`get_drive_id_by_name(headers: dict, site_id: str, drive_name: str) -> str | None`

Resolve a drive ID by its display name. The match is case-sensitive and exact. Returns `None` if no drive matches or the underlying request fails.

```python
drive_id = get_drive_id_by_name(headers, site_id, "01_Whitecap")
```

## SharePoint — Delta Functions

### get_changed_items

`get_changed_items(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None = None, *, drive_id: str | None = None, delta_link: str | None = None, page_size: int | None = None) -> tuple[list[dict], str | None]`

Find the files that changed in a drive since the last call. This exists because a
driveItem change notification carries **no item ID, name, or path** — Graph sends
null resource data for that resource — so a notification tells you *that*
something changed in a drive, not what.

Returns `(files, delta_link)`:

- **First call** — omit `delta_link`. Graph returns every item currently in the
  drive as a baseline, not a change set. Store the returned link.
- **Every later call** — pass the stored link back as `delta_link`. Graph then
  returns only what changed since that link was issued. `site_id`, `drive_id`,
  and `page_size` are ignored in this mode, because the link already encodes
  them. Store the new link even when `files` is empty.

`files` contains only items carrying a `file` facet, with folder items and
deletions dropped. Raises `ValueError` if neither `site_id` nor `drive_id` nor
`delta_link` is given.

```python
# First call: baseline, plus a link to use next time.
files, delta_link = get_changed_items(credentials, site_id)
save_delta_link(delta_link)

# Later, on a notification or a schedule:
files, delta_link = get_changed_items(credentials, delta_link=load_delta_link())
for item in files:
    print(item["name"], item["id"])
save_delta_link(delta_link)
```

Added in 1.15.1.

## SharePoint — File Functions

All file helpers accept an optional keyword-only `drive_id` argument. When omitted (the default), operations target the site's default document library. When provided, operations target the specified drive — useful for sites that host multiple libraries. `site_id` is optional when `drive_id` is given directly.

Most also take a keyword-only `item_id` — and `destination_id` on `move_file` and `copy_file` — to address an item or folder by its Graph ID instead of a path. Where both are supplied the ID wins and the path is ignored, so the path argument may be `None`. Added in 1.15.1.

### list_folder

`list_folder(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None, folder_path: str | None = None, *, drive_id: str | None = None, item_id: str | None = None, page_size: int | None = None) -> list[dict]`

List the immediate children of a folder. Follows `@odata.nextLink` across all pages — results are not silently truncated at the first page. Pass `folder_path="/"` (or `""`) to list the root of the drive.

```python
# Default drive
files = list_folder(headers, site_id, "/Shared Documents/My Folder")

# Non-default drive, tuned page size
files = list_folder(headers, site_id, "/2026/04", drive_id=drive_id, page_size=500)

# Root of a non-default drive
files = list_folder(headers, site_id, "/", drive_id=drive_id)
```

### get_item_metadata

`get_item_metadata(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None, file_path: str | None, *, drive_id: str | None = None, item_id: str | None = None) -> dict`

Retrieve the metadata of an item on a SharePoint site, by path or by ID. When the
item is a file, the returned metadata also carries `name_no_extension` and
`extension`, split from `name`. Raises `ValueError` if neither `file_path` nor
`item_id` is given.

```python
get_item_metadata(headers, site_id, "/Shared Documents/My Folder/file.txt")
get_item_metadata(headers, site_id, "/2026/04/20/invoice.pdf", drive_id=drive_id)
get_item_metadata(headers, site_id, None, item_id=item_id)
```

**Renamed in 1.15.1**, from `get_file_metadata`.

### get_file_content

`get_file_content(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None, file_path: str | None, *, drive_id: str | None = None, item_id: str | None = None) -> bytes`

Retrieve the raw content of a file. Two addressing modes are supported:

- **Path-based** (SharePoint) — pass `site_id` and `file_path`. An optional `drive_id` targets a specific document library; omitting it uses the site's default drive.
- **ID-based** (OneDrive) — pass `drive_id` and `item_id`. Bypasses path resolution entirely.

Raises `ValueError` when neither mode is fully satisfied: `item_id` given without `drive_id`, or `site_id` and `file_path` not both present. Raises `requests.RequestException` on HTTP errors or exhausted retries.

```python
# Path-based, site's default drive
get_file_content(headers, site_id, "/Shared Documents/My Folder/file.txt")

# Path-based, explicit document library
get_file_content(headers, site_id, "/2026/04/20/invoice.pdf", drive_id=drive_id)

# ID-based, when the drive and item IDs are already known
get_file_content(headers, None, None, drive_id=drive_id, item_id=item_id)
```

> **Note:** the former `get_file_content_by_id` helper was merged into `get_file_content`. Pass `drive_id` and `item_id` instead.

### upload_file

`upload_file(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None, file_path: str | None, filename: str, content: bytes | bytearray | memoryview | str, conflict_behavior: str = 'rename', *, drive_id: str | None = None, item_id: str | None = None) -> dict`

Upload a file to a SharePoint site. Parent folders do not need to exist beforehand. The `conflict_behavior` parameter controls what happens when a file with the same name already exists:

- **rename**: *(Default)* Renames the uploaded file to avoid conflict.
- **replace**: Overwrites the existing file.
- **fail**: Fails the upload if a file with the same name exists.

```python
upload_file(headers, site_id, "/Shared Documents/Reports", "report.xlsx", file_bytes, conflict_behavior="replace")
upload_file(headers, site_id, "/2026/04/20", "invoice.pdf", file_bytes, drive_id=drive_id)
```

### upload_multiple_files

`upload_multiple_files(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None, files: list[tuple[str | None, str, bytes | bytearray | memoryview | str]], conflict_behavior: str = "rename", *, drive_id: str | None = None, item_id: str | None = None) -> list[dict]`

Upload several files to the same SharePoint folder in parallel, one thread per
file, each through `upload_file`. Takes `(file_path, filename, content)` tuples
and returns the Graph responses in the same order.

Every file is attempted: all uploads are in flight before any result is known,
so one failure cannot cancel the others. **If any file fails, an
`ExceptionGroup` is raised** holding one `requests.RequestException` per failed
file, each carrying a note naming its file, and the successful responses are
discarded. Re-running the whole batch is the intended recovery and is safe with
`conflict_behavior="replace"`.

Every exception is collected, not only `requests.RequestException` and `TypeError`: one left uncaught in a worker thread would never reach the caller at all.

Changed in 1.15.0, reverted in 1.15.1, restored in 1.15.3 — see [Breaking changes in 1.15.x](#breaking-changes-in-115x).

```python
try:
    upload_multiple_files(
        credentials,
        site_id,
        [("/Shared Documents/Staging", "a.csv", a_bytes),
         ("/Shared Documents/Staging", "b.csv", b_bytes)],
        conflict_behavior="replace",
    )
except* requests.RequestException as failures:
    for error in failures.exceptions:
        logger.error("upload failed: %s", error)
```

### download_file

`download_file(headers: dict | GraphCredentials, site_id: str | None, file_path: str | None, download_folder: Path, *, drive_id: str | None = None, item_id: str | None = None, filename: str | None = None) -> Path`

Download a file from a SharePoint site into `download_folder`. Returns the `Path` of the downloaded file on success. Raises `requests.RequestException` on HTTP errors or exhausted retries.

```python
