## Notification Parsing

### get_resource_context

`get_resource_context(notification: dict) -> dict`

Turn one entry from a change or lifecycle notification payload (an item from its
`value` list) into the identifiers needed to act on the resource behind it. It
classifies the notification with
[`get_resource_type`](#get_resource_type) and runs the parser for that type.

Every result carries `resource_type` and `subscription_id`. The remaining fields
depend on the type, and a field is **omitted rather than set to `None`** when it
could not be determined:

| `resource_type` | Example resource | Other fields |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `mail` | `users/u1/mailFolders/mf1/messages/m1` | `user_id`, `mail_folder_id`, `message_id` |
| `calendar` | `users/u1/calendars/c1/events/e1` | `user_id`, `calendar_id`, `event_id` |
| `contacts` | `users/u1/contactFolders/cf1/contacts/c1` | `user_id`, `contact_folder_id`, `contact_id` |
| `sharepoint` | `sites/s1/drives/d1/root` | `site_id`, `drive_id`, `item_id` (always `None`), `note` |
| `drive` | `users/u1/drive/root` | `user_id`, `item_id` (always `None`), `note` |
| `teams` | `teams/t1/channels/c1/messages/m1/replies/r1` | `team_id`, `channel_id`, `message_id`, `reply_id` |
| `teams` | `chats/c1/messages/m1` | `chat_id`, `message_id` |
| `presence` | `communications/presences/u1` | `user_id` |
| `print` | `print/printers/p1/jobs/j1` | `printer_id`, `job_id` |
| `print` | `print/printTaskDefinitions/td1/tasks/t1` | `task_definition_id`, `task_id` |
| `todo` | `users/u1/todo/lists/l1/tasks/t1` | `user_id`, `list_id`, `task_id` |
| `security` | `security/alerts/a1` | `alert_id` |
| `copilot` | `copilot/users/u1/interactionHistory` | `user_id`, `interaction_id` |
| `directory` | `groups/g1` | `collection`, `object_id` |
| `default` | anything unrecognised | `resource` |

The two driveItem rows are the reason
[`get_changed_items`](#get_changed_items) exists: `item_id` is always `None`
there, and `note` says so. Run a delta query on the drive to find what changed.

```python
for notification in payload["value"]:
    context = get_resource_context(notification)
    if context["resource_type"] == "mail":
        message = get_email_metadata(credentials, context["user_id"], context["message_id"])
```

Added in 1.15.1.

## Subscription Functions

### get_resource_type

`get_resource_type(resource: str) -> str`

Classify a Graph resource path, returning one of `mail`, `calendar`, `contacts`,
`drive`, `sharepoint`, `directory`, `teams`, `presence`, `print`, `todo`,
`security`, `copilot`, or `default` when nothing matches. The result picks both
the subscription lifetime used by `create_subscription` and the parser used by
[`get_resource_context`](#get_resource_context).

Matching walks an ordered, most-specific-first list over the path's segments,
because several resource shapes nest a specific collection under a generic scope
segment that is also a key — `users/{id}/todo/...` is `todo`, not `directory`,
and `teams/{id}/channels/{id}/messages/{id}` is `teams`, not `mail`.

```python
get_resource_type("users/u1/messages")                      # "mail"
get_resource_type("teams/t1/channels/c1/messages/m1")       # "teams"
get_resource_type("users/u1/todo/lists/l1/tasks/t1")        # "todo"
```

Public since 1.15.1; the four nesting cases above were fixed in 1.15.2.

### create_subscription

`create_subscription(headers: dict, notification_url: str, resource_type: str, resource: str, change_type: str, client_state: str, *, include_resource_data: bool = False, encryption_certificate: str | None = None, encryption_certificate_id: str | None = None) -> dict`

Create a new Microsoft Graph webhook subscription and return the created subscription object. The `notification_url` is automatically suffixed with `/api/graph` for notifications and `/api/lifecycle` for lifecycle events. Expiration is calculated automatically based on `resource_type`.

Set `include_resource_data=True` to have Graph deliver the changed resource's data inside the notification payload rather than just a change pointer. Graph encrypts that payload, so this requires both `encryption_certificate` (the base64-encoded public key it should encrypt with) and `encryption_certificate_id` (your own identifier for that certificate, echoed back in each notification so the matching private key can be selected). Passing `include_resource_data=True` without both raises `ValueError`. This is the path needed for app-only subscriptions on richer resources such as Teams chat messages.

```python
create_subscription(
    headers,
    notification_url="https://myapp.example.com",
    resource_type="mail",
    resource="users/user@contoso.com/messages",
    change_type="created",
    client_state="my-secret-state",
)
```

With resource data included in the notification:

```python
create_subscription(
    headers,
    notification_url="https://myapp.example.com",
    resource_type="teams",
    resource="chats/getAllMessages",
    change_type="created",
    client_state="my-secret-state",
    include_resource_data=True,
    encryption_certificate=base64_public_cert,
    encryption_certificate_id="my-cert-v1",
)
```

